> >>   (improves cast rates on hard spells)
>
> Excellent! Eventually the robe will become your cloak. Trust me when
> you lose the metal armor and shields, get a helm of brilliance,
> max out your wisdom using gain abilities and put on the robe, you'll
> become an excellent spellcaster.

> Note that I'm practicing what I preach. Everything is fixed. I'm using both
> the helm and the robe.

> R - a blessed fireproof +3 pair of leather gloves (being worn)

Unless you found them that way or had a run-in with a disenchanter (or
wished for them--dubious since you'd go with dex then) fixing was a bad
choice here.

As it is now, they're 4 AC.  Blessed ?ea would make them either 5AC
until damaged, then 4AC, or it'd make them 6AC until damaged, then 5AC.
 So even if you only got one extra AC, it'd make them better in the
short term and as good as fixed permanently.  Two points would be a
permanent win.

Indeed, for most things fixed isn't worth the effort unless you have
tons of extra ?ea.  The only things I usually bother fixing are
weapons (fixing water-walking boots for lava is nice, and fixing robes
is worth more than fixing most items).  But until I've enchanted
everything to +4 and have 2-3 ?ea in reserve in case I decide to switch
in another piece of armor, I don't bother fixing things.  And by that
point, it's usually not even worth the turns and effort.



